.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt LINKER_SET 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm linker_set
.Nd linker mechanism to group together statically declared objects
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/cdefs.h
.In sys/linker_set.h
.Ss Add entry to a set
.Fn TEXT_SET set sym
.Fn DATA_SET set sym
.Fn DATA_WSET set sym
.Ss Set declaration and manipulation
.Fn SET_DECLARE set ptype
.Fn SET_BEGIN set
.Fn SET_LIMIT set
.Fn SET_FOREACH pvar set
.Fn SET_ITEM set i
.Fn SET_COUNT set
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The linker set mechanism allows the declaration of global sets of objects.
Items can be added to a set from anywhere by using the
.Fn *_SET
macros.
Then, the linker collects the items and stores a set as an array of pointers.
For ELF, this is done by constructing a separate section for each set.
.Pp
The
.Fn TEXT_SET
macro appends the executable symbol
.Fa sym
to the given linker
.Fa set .
.Pp
The
.Fn DATA_SET
macro appends the data symbol
.Fa sym
to the given linker
.Fa set .
.Pp
The
.Fn DATA_WSET
macro is similar to the previous one, but the linker set is treated as writable.
.Pp
The
.Fn SET_DECLARE
macro initializes the given linker
.Fa set ,
and should be used before accessing it.
.Pp
The
.Fn SET_BEGIN
and
.Fn SET_LIMIT
macros respectively calculate the pointer to the first and last element in the
.Fa set .
.Pp
The
.Fn SET_FOREACH
macro can be used as a for loop statement to iterate the items in the
.Fa set .
The
.Fa pvar
argument should be declared as
.Ql type **
since sets contain the addresses of the elements.
.Pp
The
.Fn SET_ITEM
macro retrieves the item in the i-th position.
No bounds checking is performed.
.Pp
The
.Fn SET_COUNT
macro calculates the number of items in the
.Fa set .
.Sh EXAMPLES
A new linker set can be declared and manipulated like this:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct var {
	int val;
};

SET_DECLARE(vars_set, struct var);

struct var **ptr;
SET_FOREACH(ptr, vars_set) {
	printf("val is %d\en", (*ptr)->val);
}
.Ed
.Pp
then new items can be added to the set from other locations:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static struct var myvar = {
	.val = 42
};
DATA_SET(vars_set, myvar);
.Ed
